{
  "summary": "The premise that CC posts usage directly to FI-CA, thereby eliminating the need for CI, is architecturally incorrect for standard SAP BRIM postpaid billing. In the canonical architecture, CC rates usage to produce charged items, which are transferred to CI as billable items. CI then performs billing and invoicing, and it is CI that posts the resulting receivables to FI-CA. While simplified architectures may bypass CC for simple recurring charges, CI remains a mandatory component for billing and receivables management, and bypassing CI to post directly from external events is explicitly identified as skipping necessary controls.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Architectural Correction: The Role of CI in the Billing Flow",
      "module": "CI"
    },
    {
      "title": "Standard Data Flow: CC to CI to FI-CA",
      "module": "CC"
    },
    {
      "title": "FI-CA Integration and Posting Mechanics",
      "module": "FI-CA"
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "text": "Creating FI-CA receivables directly from external usage events skips the controls that CI exists to provide, indicating that direct posting from usage sources to FI-CA is not the standard or recommended architectural path.",
      "citations": [
        "2faf0dd5-e390-41e5-b3d5-b8680649fdbc"
      ],
      "identifiers_used": [],
      "module": "CI",
      "support": "direct"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "text": "In the standard postpaid flow, CC rates usage and sends billable items to CI, while FI-CA holds the receivables; CI is the intermediary that processes these items into invoices.",
      "citations": [
        "59619da2-d0eb-471f-b7b6-e219f766cb0d"
      ],
      "identifiers_used": [],
      "module": "CC",
      "support": "direct"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "text": "The end-to-end processing sequence involves CC producing charged items as billable items for CI, which then bills and invoices them, posting the receivable to FI-CA.",
      "citations": [
        "5b7d6143-1027-43ce-99e9-a1e007639280",
        "843f96e1-be23-4e2d-a193-9b9b4ae98b0d"
      ],
      "identifiers_used": [],
      "module": "CC",
      "support": "direct"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "text": "CI posts through FI-CA, where business partner and contract account hold the customer's receivables; invoicing creates an FI-CA posting document with the receivable on the contract account and revenue and tax lines.",
      "citations": [
        "24b11999-842f-41d4-9dac-c9148e3d56dc"
      ],
      "identifiers_used": [],
      "module": "CI",
      "support": "direct"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "text": "Even in simplified architectures where CC is omitted for fixed recurring pricing, CI and FI-CA cannot be omitted, as they are required for billing and receivables.",
      "citations": [
        "f3441dc5-fe25-4a9d-919f-334ad2f0538f"
      ],
      "identifiers_used": [],
      "module": "SOM",
      "support": "direct"
    }]}

